.TH HYPERDOC 1
.SH NAME
hyperdoc \- linked-document hypertext browser
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wm/hyperdoc
.RI [ docA
.RI [ docB ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Hyperdoc
is a work-alike of Ted Nelson's Xanadu/hypermedia idea and his
.I "Parallel Textface"
(see
.IR "Computer Lib/Dream Machines" ,
1974): two scrollable text panels, side by side, with a canvas strip
between them showing visible connector lines for the links currently
on screen.
.PP
.I Docs
default to
.B /man/1/intro
and
.BR /man/1/sh .
.PP
Select a span of text in either panel (drag with button 1, matching
the usual Tk text-selection gesture) then use the
.B Link
button to record a named link between the two selections. Links persist
in
.B /lib/hyperdoc/links
(tab-separated: document, start index, end index, document, start
index, end index, label) and are reloaded on every launch.
.PP
A linked span is drawn with a highlight; clicking it scrolls the other
panel to its partner (Nelson's
.IR "derivative motion" ).
If a link's partner is currently scrolled off screen, its connector is
drawn as a broken stub pointing toward whichever edge the partner lies
past, rather than a dangling arrow to nowhere.
.PP
The
.B "Sync scroll"
checkbutton turns on a stronger form of the Parallel Textface: whichever
panel is scrolled becomes the driver, and the other panel is snapped to
the nearest link boundary in the driver's current view, so the two
documents track each other continuously rather than only on a click.
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/hyperdoc/links
.TP
.B /lib/hyperdoc/links
Persisted link records, tab-separated.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/hyperdoc.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR wm (1),
.IR man (1)
